WEBVTT

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:10.177
Around 1350, a Mamluk secretary kept a
book of the ciphers his friends sent him.

00:00:10.177 --> 00:00:12.669
About 150 invented alphabets.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.118
This is the last. Under it he wrote:
I solved it at once, in his presence.

00:00:18.118 --> 00:00:20.677
But the page with his answer is lost.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.687
The AI writes down every sign.
89 of them, in 22 shapes.

00:00:30.687 --> 00:00:35.778
The stroke with three dots is not
a letter. It divides the words.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:40.855
That is far too few for statistics.
This one has to be reasoned out.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:49.135
The first clue is already on the page.
A reader wrote five letters in red,

00:00:49.135 --> 00:00:50.768
above the opening signs.

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:56.832
Then a group of four signs appears three
times. It can only be the word for eyelids.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:03.177
Arabic verse keeps a strict beat. The
first line only fits the long metre, ṭawīl,

00:01:03.177 --> 00:01:06.315
if its last three words mean: love has made.

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:09.592
One guess leads nowhere, and is corrected.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.605
The last signs fall out of single
words. One damaged word stays unread.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.011
Letter by letter, the signs become words.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.807
22 signs. 22 letters.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:35.349
Is it right? The metre holds. The rhyme
holds. And no sign is read two ways.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.161
Could another key fit?

00:01:42.161 --> 00:01:47.339
A search through classical Arabic
found 21,000 keys that make real words.

00:01:47.339 --> 00:01:51.383
For the signs used more than
once, they come down to six.

00:01:51.383 --> 00:01:53.795
The rhyme and the sense leave one.

00:01:54.200 --> 00:01:59.952
After the reading, the second verse turned
up in a 13th-century history of Aleppo.

00:01:59.952 --> 00:02:00.934
Word for word.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.623
Then a specialist read it again. Alan
Elbaum, of the Princeton Geniza Project,

00:02:06.623 --> 00:02:11.042
sent eight corrections: the sender's
name, one letter of the poem,

00:02:11.042 --> 00:02:16.532
and one word the AI had filled in from the
metre, which the page does not support.

00:02:16.532 --> 00:02:18.072
The key did not change.

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:21.076
It is a love poem.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:25.246
وناعسةِ الأجفانِ قد جعلَ الهوى

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:29.720
[…] مهجتي مثوًى وخدّي لها أرضا

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.255
Many a girl with drowsy eyelids: love
has made my heart [her] dwelling,

00:02:35.255 --> 00:02:37.717
and my cheek the ground beneath her.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.011
سهرتُ وأجفاني صِحاحٌ ولم أنمْ

00:02:42.511 --> 00:02:46.226
ونامتْ ولم تسهرْ وأجفانُها مرضى

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.816
I lay awake, though my eyelids are
sound, and did not sleep. She slept,

00:02:51.816 --> 00:02:55.348
and did not lie awake,
though her eyelids are sick.

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:02.059
One word is still open. The ink shows
more letters than the metre's guess.

00:03:02.059 --> 00:03:03.109
Can you read it?

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:12.944
A love poem, hidden in a
cipher, for a friend to solve.

